-- New stories: 3 Singapore stories covering the February 2026
-- Committee of Supply confirmation extending GST InvoiceNow to all
-- GST-registered businesses by April 2031, the April 2026 milestone
-- for all new voluntary registrants, and the mid-2026 process for
-- existing businesses to confirm their specific deadline. First
-- stories for Singapore, identified via the country-by-country
-- story-count audit. Sourced to the official IRAS portal.
-- Generated by migrations/generate_singapore_stories.py.

INSERT INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2026-02-26-singapore-invoicenow-full-extension', '2026-02-26', '2026-02', 'At the Committee of Supply 2026 debate, IRAS confirmed that GST InvoiceNow will progressively extend to every GST-registered business by April 2031 — bringing roughly 90,000 additional businesses onto the network — with new financial support grants of up to SGD 5,000 for early adopters.', '<h3>🇸🇬 IRAS confirms GST InvoiceNow extends to all GST-registered businesses by April 2031</h3>
<p>At the Ministry of Finance''s Committee of Supply 2026 debate, the government and the Inland Revenue Authority of Singapore confirmed a genuinely significant expansion: the GST InvoiceNow requirement — until now limited to new voluntary GST registrants — will extend progressively to every GST-registered business in the country by April 2031. This is expected to bring approximately 90,000 additional businesses onto a network that currently serves over 63,000 users, rolling out in phases from April 2028 for new compulsory registrants and smaller existing businesses through to April 2031 for the largest existing GST-registered businesses.</p>
<p>To ease the transition, the government announced meaningful financial support alongside the mandate expansion: a new grant of up to SGD 1,000 for SMEs to offset the operational costs of adopting InvoiceNow-Ready Solutions, on top of the existing Productivity Solutions Grant covering up to 50% of eligible software subscription costs. A separate grant of up to SGD 5,000 is available specifically for early adopters — businesses that connect ahead of their actual mandatory deadline rather than waiting until the last possible moment.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> if your business is an existing GST-registered entity not yet using InvoiceNow, this announcement is your genuine signal to start planning rather than waiting for a deadline that felt distant — with roughly 90,000 businesses being brought into scope over the coming years, demand for accredited solution providers and Access Points will likely increase as deadlines approach. The early-adopter grant specifically rewards moving before you''re required to, which is worth weighing against simply waiting for your own mandatory date to arrive.</p>', 'https://www.iras.gov.sg/taxes/goods-services-tax-(gst)/gst-invoicenow-requirement', 1);
INSERT INTO story_countries (story_id, country_id) SELECT '2026-02-26-singapore-invoicenow-full-extension', id FROM countries WHERE name_en = 'Singapore';
INSERT INTO story_translations (story_id, lang, title, summary, html) VALUES ('2026-02-26-singapore-invoicenow-full-extension', 'en', 'IRAS confirms GST InvoiceNow extends to all GST-registered businesses by April 2031', 'At the Committee of Supply 2026 debate, IRAS confirmed that GST InvoiceNow will progressively extend to every GST-registered business by April 2031 — bringing roughly 90,000 additional businesses onto the network — with new financial support grants of up to SGD 5,000 for early adopters.', '<h3>🇸🇬 IRAS confirms GST InvoiceNow extends to all GST-registered businesses by April 2031</h3>
<p>At the Ministry of Finance''s Committee of Supply 2026 debate, the government and the Inland Revenue Authority of Singapore confirmed a genuinely significant expansion: the GST InvoiceNow requirement — until now limited to new voluntary GST registrants — will extend progressively to every GST-registered business in the country by April 2031. This is expected to bring approximately 90,000 additional businesses onto a network that currently serves over 63,000 users, rolling out in phases from April 2028 for new compulsory registrants and smaller existing businesses through to April 2031 for the largest existing GST-registered businesses.</p>
<p>To ease the transition, the government announced meaningful financial support alongside the mandate expansion: a new grant of up to SGD 1,000 for SMEs to offset the operational costs of adopting InvoiceNow-Ready Solutions, on top of the existing Productivity Solutions Grant covering up to 50% of eligible software subscription costs. A separate grant of up to SGD 5,000 is available specifically for early adopters — businesses that connect ahead of their actual mandatory deadline rather than waiting until the last possible moment.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> if your business is an existing GST-registered entity not yet using InvoiceNow, this announcement is your genuine signal to start planning rather than waiting for a deadline that felt distant — with roughly 90,000 businesses being brought into scope over the coming years, demand for accredited solution providers and Access Points will likely increase as deadlines approach. The early-adopter grant specifically rewards moving before you''re required to, which is worth weighing against simply waiting for your own mandatory date to arrive.</p>');
INSERT INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2026-04-01-singapore-voluntary-registrants-mandatory', '2026-04-01', '2026-04', 'From today, every new voluntary GST registrant in Singapore — regardless of incorporation date or business structure — must adopt InvoiceNow, extending a requirement that previously applied only to newly incorporated businesses registering within six months of formation.', '<h3>🇸🇬 All new voluntary GST registrants must now use InvoiceNow, regardless of business structure</h3>
<p>The GST InvoiceNow Requirement''s initial phase, effective from November 2025, covered a genuinely narrow population: only newly incorporated businesses that voluntarily registered for GST within six months of their incorporation date. From today, that scope widens meaningfully — every new voluntary GST registrant must adopt InvoiceNow, regardless of incorporation date or business structure, covering limited liability partnerships, sole proprietorships, and partnerships alongside standard companies.</p>
<p>Worth being clear about what InvoiceNow actually requires operationally: GST-registered businesses in scope must transmit prescribed invoice data directly to IRAS using an IMDA-accredited InvoiceNow-Ready Solution or through an accredited Access Point, built on the Peppol network using the PINT-SG specification — Singapore''s Peppol International format, itself an upgrade of the earlier SG Peppol BIS 3.0 UBL specification designed to support seamless cross-border transactions. A genuinely important point that''s easy to miss: InvoiceNow doesn''t change what must legally appear on a GST tax invoice — the same 11 mandatory fields IRAS requires still apply regardless of transmission method.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> if you''re voluntarily registering for GST from today onward, regardless of your business structure, factor InvoiceNow onboarding into your registration planning from the outset rather than treating it as a separate, later step. IRAS notes that onboarding time varies — allow 3 to 12 months for system connection depending on your existing accounting or ERP solution''s readiness, so starting early matters even though the mandate itself begins immediately upon registration.</p>', 'https://www.iras.gov.sg/taxes/goods-services-tax-(gst)/gst-invoicenow-requirement', 1);
INSERT INTO story_countries (story_id, country_id) SELECT '2026-04-01-singapore-voluntary-registrants-mandatory', id FROM countries WHERE name_en = 'Singapore';
INSERT INTO story_translations (story_id, lang, title, summary, html) VALUES ('2026-04-01-singapore-voluntary-registrants-mandatory', 'en', 'All new voluntary GST registrants must now use InvoiceNow, regardless of business structure', 'From today, every new voluntary GST registrant in Singapore — regardless of incorporation date or business structure — must adopt InvoiceNow, extending a requirement that previously applied only to newly incorporated businesses registering within six months of formation.', '<h3>🇸🇬 All new voluntary GST registrants must now use InvoiceNow, regardless of business structure</h3>
<p>The GST InvoiceNow Requirement''s initial phase, effective from November 2025, covered a genuinely narrow population: only newly incorporated businesses that voluntarily registered for GST within six months of their incorporation date. From today, that scope widens meaningfully — every new voluntary GST registrant must adopt InvoiceNow, regardless of incorporation date or business structure, covering limited liability partnerships, sole proprietorships, and partnerships alongside standard companies.</p>
<p>Worth being clear about what InvoiceNow actually requires operationally: GST-registered businesses in scope must transmit prescribed invoice data directly to IRAS using an IMDA-accredited InvoiceNow-Ready Solution or through an accredited Access Point, built on the Peppol network using the PINT-SG specification — Singapore''s Peppol International format, itself an upgrade of the earlier SG Peppol BIS 3.0 UBL specification designed to support seamless cross-border transactions. A genuinely important point that''s easy to miss: InvoiceNow doesn''t change what must legally appear on a GST tax invoice — the same 11 mandatory fields IRAS requires still apply regardless of transmission method.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> if you''re voluntarily registering for GST from today onward, regardless of your business structure, factor InvoiceNow onboarding into your registration planning from the outset rather than treating it as a separate, later step. IRAS notes that onboarding time varies — allow 3 to 12 months for system connection depending on your existing accounting or ERP solution''s readiness, so starting early matters even though the mandate itself begins immediately upon registration.</p>');
INSERT INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2026-06-15-singapore-existing-business-timeline-check', '2026-06-15', '2026-06', 'IRAS has committed to informing all GST-registered businesses that registered before 2026 of their specific mandatory InvoiceNow implementation date by mid-2026, based on total annual supplies — businesses can also self-check now using IRAS''s own Excel calculator tool.', '<h3>🇸🇬 Existing GST-registered businesses: your specific InvoiceNow deadline is being confirmed this year</h3>
<p>With the GST InvoiceNow mandate now confirmed to extend to every GST-registered business by April 2031, a genuinely practical question follows immediately for businesses already registered before 2026: exactly when does your own mandatory deadline fall within that multi-year window? IRAS has committed to directly informing these businesses of their specific implementation date by mid-2026, based on total annual supplies — standard-rated, zero-rated, and exempt supplies made across prescribed accounting periods ending in calendar year 2025.</p>
<p>Businesses don''t need to wait passively for that formal notification, though. IRAS has published a self-help Excel calculator specifically designed to let existing GST-registered businesses determine their own applicable implementation date directly, without waiting on official correspondence. Given the rollout spans from April 2028 through April 2031 depending on business size, knowing your specific position within that range — rather than assuming you''re either at the very start or very end of it — genuinely changes how much lead time you actually have for planning.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> don''t wait for IRAS''s formal notification if you''d rather know now — use the self-help calculator to get a working estimate of your own deadline based on your actual 2025 supply figures. Given onboarding can take anywhere from 3 to 12 months depending on your existing systems, knowing whether you''re facing an April 2028 or April 2031 deadline materially changes whether this is something to start now or something that can genuinely wait.</p>', 'https://www.iras.gov.sg/taxes/goods-services-tax-(gst)/gst-invoicenow-requirement', 1);
INSERT INTO story_countries (story_id, country_id) SELECT '2026-06-15-singapore-existing-business-timeline-check', id FROM countries WHERE name_en = 'Singapore';
INSERT INTO story_translations (story_id, lang, title, summary, html) VALUES ('2026-06-15-singapore-existing-business-timeline-check', 'en', 'Existing GST-registered businesses: your specific InvoiceNow deadline is being confirmed this year', 'IRAS has committed to informing all GST-registered businesses that registered before 2026 of their specific mandatory InvoiceNow implementation date by mid-2026, based on total annual supplies — businesses can also self-check now using IRAS''s own Excel calculator tool.', '<h3>🇸🇬 Existing GST-registered businesses: your specific InvoiceNow deadline is being confirmed this year</h3>
<p>With the GST InvoiceNow mandate now confirmed to extend to every GST-registered business by April 2031, a genuinely practical question follows immediately for businesses already registered before 2026: exactly when does your own mandatory deadline fall within that multi-year window? IRAS has committed to directly informing these businesses of their specific implementation date by mid-2026, based on total annual supplies — standard-rated, zero-rated, and exempt supplies made across prescribed accounting periods ending in calendar year 2025.</p>
<p>Businesses don''t need to wait passively for that formal notification, though. IRAS has published a self-help Excel calculator specifically designed to let existing GST-registered businesses determine their own applicable implementation date directly, without waiting on official correspondence. Given the rollout spans from April 2028 through April 2031 depending on business size, knowing your specific position within that range — rather than assuming you''re either at the very start or very end of it — genuinely changes how much lead time you actually have for planning.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> don''t wait for IRAS''s formal notification if you''d rather know now — use the self-help calculator to get a working estimate of your own deadline based on your actual 2025 supply figures. Given onboarding can take anywhere from 3 to 12 months depending on your existing systems, knowing whether you''re facing an April 2028 or April 2031 deadline materially changes whether this is something to start now or something that can genuinely wait.</p>');
